  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🎮 Xbox Gamertag Generator",
      "tagline": "Gamertags that fit Xbox's 12 characters, spaces and all: Quiet Comet, Rook Nine, xXHavocXx."
    },
    "intro": "Xbox gamertags can have spaces in them, which most platforms don't allow, so a tag can read like a real name or a callsign. This **Xbox gamertag generator** makes tags of 12 characters or fewer in four styles (two words with a space, squad callsigns, funny British tags and old-school xX…Xx tags) and can mix in a word or name of your own. It's an unofficial tool, not made by or connected with Microsoft or Xbox.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Eight good Xbox gamertags",
        "content": "<p>All eight came from the generator, two from each style:</p><ul><li><strong>Quiet Comet</strong>: two short words with a space, easy to say in party chat.</li><li><strong>Brisk Heron</strong>: a calm animal name that won't date.</li><li><strong>Rook Nine</strong>: a callsign, for players who like to sound like a squad on the radio.</li><li><strong>Kilo Actual</strong>: NATO alphabet plus \"Actual\", the radio word for the person in charge.</li><li><strong>Soggy Scone</strong>: funny and very British.</li><li><strong>No Idea Mate</strong>: a whole phrase that fits in exactly 12 characters.</li><li><strong>xXHavocXx</strong>: the xX…Xx wrapper that was everywhere in the Xbox 360 years.</li><li><strong>iiFrosty</strong>: another old-school style, with the double i in front.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Xbox gamertag rules",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Length:</strong> a gamertag can be up to 12 characters, and spaces count.</li><li><strong>Spaces:</strong> allowed, so <em>Quiet Comet</em> works as it is.</li><li><strong>Taken names:</strong> since 2019 you can choose a gamertag even if someone else has it. Xbox adds a # and a number after it (Quiet Comet#4821) so everyone stays unique, and shows the number smaller than the name.</li><li><strong>The number:</strong> Xbox picks it, not you. The name and number together can be up to 16 characters, # included, so a full 12-character tag gets a 3-digit number and shorter tags usually get 4 digits.</li><li><strong>Older tags:</strong> players who already had a gamertag before the change kept it with no number, and those classic tags could be up to 15 characters.</li></ul><p>Every tag here is 12 characters or fewer, uses only letters, numbers and spaces, and starts with a letter. Xbox also accepts letters from many other alphabets and accents, which this generator leaves out.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Spaces, callsigns and xX tags",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Two words:</strong> because Xbox allows spaces, an adjective and a noun (Lost Otter, Brisk Heron) reads cleanly in a lobby and on a scoreboard. Keep each word short: two six-letter words with a space make 13, one too many.</li><li><strong>Callsigns:</strong> they come from military radio, and squad shooters made them familiar. A NATO letter or a bird of prey plus a number word (Rook Nine, Osprey Lead) sounds like it belongs in a squad, and friends can take one each.</li><li><strong>Funny:</strong> British food and grumpy adjectives (Soggy Scone, Damp Welly), or a whole phrase like No Idea Mate.</li><li><strong>Old-school:</strong> xX…Xx, ii and \"Its\" in front, or HD on the end, are the styles people remember from the Xbox 360 years. Wearing one now is half nostalgia, half joke.</li></ul><p>Want a number on the end? Choose <strong>With a number</strong> for tags like Otter 99 or Rook 27. The numbers are never 69, 420 or other numbers with a second meaning.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Before you change your gamertag",
        "content": "<p>The tags here are free to use, but check yours is available first: try it in the Xbox app or on the Xbox website before you change your gamertag. If the plain name is taken, Xbox adds a number, so decide whether you're happy with Quiet Comet#4821 or would rather choose a tag that's still free on its own.</p><p>If you stream as well, the <a href=\"twitch-name-generator.html\">Twitch Name Generator</a> makes names that fit Twitch's rules (no spaces there), and the <a href=\"psn-name-generator.html\">PSN Name Generator</a> does the same for PlayStation. For a name that works on any platform, try the <a href=\"gamertag-generator.html\">Gamertag Generator</a>, and for younger players the <a href=\"kids-gamertag-generator.html\">Kids' Gamertag Generator</a>.</p>"
      }
    ],
